1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.602
Now that you have exposed your agent as an A2A server,

2
00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:05.271
you'll need an A2A client to send it requests.

3
00:00:05.472 --> 00:00:07.474
And that's what you'll build in this lesson.

4
00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:10.977
An A2A client that communicates with your A2A policy agent.

5
00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:14.981
Let's have some fun. Let's start by importing the libraries.

6
00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:17.851
In this case, we're going to

7
00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:20.453
be adding in the A2A client objects

8
00:00:20.754 --> 00:00:22.956
and some of the ones that we've used before.

9
00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:25.125
Next, I'll run the same block from

10
00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:27.260
the last lesson to open up terminal one.

11
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:31.431
You might need to rerun uv run a2a_policy_agent

12
00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:33.033
to restart the A2A server if it

13
00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:34.634
timed out since you last ran it.

14
00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:37.237
The lab environment will reset after two hours.

15
00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:39.439
So in my case, the agent is still running.

16
00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:41.941
I'll load in the environment variables

17
00:00:42.042 --> 00:00:45.045
and save the host and port of the agent I'm connecting to.

18
00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:47.280
Note, you'll be reusing this same client to

19
00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:49.315
connect to another agent in a later lesson.

20
00:00:49.916 --> 00:00:51.217
So, in your copy of the

21
00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:52.719
notebook, you'll see some commented out code.

22
00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:54.921
Ignore it for now. We'll use it later.

23
00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:57.424
Next, I'll load up the user prompt that we had before.

24
00:00:57.957 --> 00:01:01.561
For this lesson, I've created a display_agent_card function

25
00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:03.396
to make it easier to see

26
00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.565
the agent card data in a notebook.

27
00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:10.737
Now, to create the A2A client.

28
00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:14.374
I'll define an httpx.AsyncClient

29
00:01:14.374 --> 00:01:16.076
which will host the connection.

30
00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:19.079
Then I'll use ClientFactory.connect

31
00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:21.081
to create the A2A client.

32
00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:24.417
I'll pass in the host, port,

33
00:01:24.417 --> 00:01:27.087
and the httpx_client that we created earlier.

34
00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:31.091
For this lesson, I'll get the agent_card directly and print it out.

35
00:01:31.091 --> 00:01:33.393
But you likely wouldn't do this in a production system.

36
00:01:33.593 --> 00:01:35.595
If you were building out a multi-agent system

37
00:01:35.595 --> 00:01:37.797
and needed to decide which agent to use,

38
00:01:38.098 --> 00:01:40.400
you would use a supervisor or coordinator agent

39
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.402
to get all the agent cards with the names and

40
00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:44.537
descriptions and decide which to use.

41
00:01:44.537 --> 00:01:46.439
We'll do that in a later lesson.

42
00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:49.709
Now, I'll wrap up the user prompt in an A2A message object,

43
00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:54.709
and I'll send it to the A2A remote agent with client.send_message.

44
00:01:59.919 --> 00:02:02.021
Next, I'll handle the responses.

45
00:02:02.622 --> 00:02:06.493
For this policy agent, you only need to handle message responses.

46
00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:08.628
So I'll check for the type,

47
00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:10.497
print out the message ID as confirmation,

48
00:02:10.597 --> 00:02:14.000
then I'll get the text content of the message with get_message_text.

49
00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.504
I'll also write an elif statement to handle tasks and artifacts,

50
00:02:17.604 --> 00:02:19.606
which you'll need for a later agent.

51
00:02:19.706 --> 00:02:23.510
In the A2A SDK, when the agent server responds to the task,

52
00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.713
it actually sends a tuple with the task itself

53
00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:30.850
and the update event. Since I'm not handling server-sent events

54
00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:33.820
in these code samples, I'll ignore the update event

55
00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:36.823
and extract the artifact from the task.

56
00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:39.225
Finally, I'll do some error checking.

57
00:02:39.225 --> 00:02:41.427
and print out the response. Let's run it.

58
00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:43.796
Okay, we've run the cell and now

59
00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:45.832
we can see everything that was printed out.

60
00:02:46.032 --> 00:02:48.368
Here we have the Agent Card details, which includes

61
00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:50.703
all the information that is within the Agent Card,

62
00:02:50.703 --> 00:02:52.338
such as the name, description of

63
00:02:52.338 --> 00:02:54.207
the agent, the version of the agent,

64
00:02:54.207 --> 00:02:56.709
the URL it's at, the protocol version.

65
00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:58.945
and all the skills.

66
00:02:58.945 --> 00:03:00.947
We can then see which prompt was sent to it.

67
00:03:00.947 --> 00:03:02.682
We can see the message ID

68
00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:04.717
printed out, and the final agent response,

69
00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:07.320
which looks pretty similar to what we had before.
